Agreed, guaspi doesn't really differentiate from the predicate logic  
foundation (if there is a "better" foundation out there, then I have yet to  
hear of it..not to say it doesn't exist).

I was under the impression that lojban was longer than english (and most  
natural languages) syllable-for-syllable, since so many things need to be  
expressed in lojban which are usually left to context in english  
(terminators, etc.)... It's true there are some lojban constructions which  
are don't translate easily into english, but I'm pretty sure I read  
somewhere "That's one of the problems with lojban; it takes a lot of  
syllables to express yourself"... If this were true, then the argument is  
that the extra syllables given by the tonal system allow you to make  
one-syllable words which stand for two-or-more syllable words, similar to  
{lei} = {lu'o le}.

Of course, I could be wrong. Does anyone know if lojban texts have  
statistically more/less syllables than their english/natural language  
equivalents? I suppose to be truly fair, those stats might need to include  
texts composed in lojban and then translated into english as well...

-Geoff






